Title:
SindriKit

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
SindriKit is a C library/framework for building Windows offensive tooling with a strong separation between “technique logic” (loader/injector/etc.) and “execution mechanics” (Win32, Native NTAPI, direct/indirect syscalls). It solves the common problem of tools being tightly coupled to a specific API call chain that becomes brittle once monitored by EDR, by using runtime-injected function-pointer API tables for memory/module/process operations. The project includes a cascading syscall pipeline with pluggable SSN resolvers and interchangeable direct vs indirect syscall invokers (including gadget-based indirect syscalls to keep return addresses in ntdll). It also emphasizes compile-time signature reduction via configurable API/string hashing (DJB2/FNV1A) with randomized seeds, plus a pre-compilation polymorphic mutation engine that mutates C and MASM code and shuffles struct layouts per build. Core components include a bounds-checked PE parser and reflective PE loader, classic shellcode/PE injection primitives, and architecture-aware dynamic FFI bridges for x86/x64 calling conventions. It’s primarily useful for red team operators, offensive tool developers, and malware researchers; defenders can also use it to understand modern modular execution abstractions and evasion-oriented build pipelines.

Technical Focus:
- Dependency-injected API abstraction tables (Win32/NTAPI/syscall profiles)
- Syscall resolution & invocation (SSN scan/sort, direct vs indirect syscalls, gadget finding)
- Reflective PE loading and bounds-checked PE parsing (PE32/PE32+)
- Process injection primitives (remote alloc/write/protect/thread/hijack patterns)
- Compile-time obfuscation (API hashing, seed randomization, silent builds)
- Polymorphic/metamorphic build mutation (opaque predicates, instruction polymorphism, struct layout shuffling)

Use Cases:
- Build loaders/injectors that can swap Win32 → NTAPI → direct syscalls without rewriting technique code
- Implement reflective PE execution with robust parsing and reduced crash surface on malformed inputs
- Develop syscall-based execution paths with fallback SSN resolution strategies
- Generate per-build unique binaries for research on static detections/signatures
- Create modular PoCs (BOF/COFF loading, classic injection variants) for controlled red team engagements

Keywords:
Windows internals, C offensive framework, dependency injection, function pointer tables, Win32 API, NTAPI, ntdll, direct syscalls, indirect syscalls, SSN resolution, syscall trampoline, gadget finder, PEB walk, EAT parsing, reflective loader, PE parser, PE32+, process injection, COFF loader, BOF symbol resolution, API hashing, DJB2, FNV1A, polymorphic mutation, metamorphic assembly, opaque predicates, CMake, MASM, stack spoofing, .pdata parsing, exception directory, silent build